    "id": 4,
    "title": "Browser History — Stack",
    "gist": "Simulate browser navigation using a stack.",
    "description": "Question: Simulate visiting three browser pages and pressing Back twice. Use a stack to store the navigation history and remove the current page when going back. Data structures: Stack<String>, following the Last In, First Out (LIFO) principle. Assumed time complexity: O(1) per push, pop, and peek operation. Space complexity: O(n). Expected navigation: Home → Search → Profile, followed by Back to Search and Back to Home. Learning goals: Stack operations, LIFO, push, pop, peek, and tracking state changes. Visualization note: Stack is shown through the supported linear item visualizer; item order follows Stack iteration order.",
    "code": "import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Stack;\nclass History {\n    final Stack<String> pages = new Stack<>();\n    ArrayList<String> toDisplay() { return new ArrayList<>(pages); }\n}\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        History history = new History();\n        visual(history, \"linear\");\n        history.pages.push(\"Home\"); point(history.pages.peek(), \"green\");\n        history.pages.push(\"Search\"); point(history.pages.peek(), \"green\");\n        history.pages.push(\"Profile\"); point(history.pages.peek(), \"green\");\n        point(history.pages.peek(), \"orange\"); history.pages.pop();\n        point(history.pages.peek(), \"orange\"); history.pages.pop();\n        System.out.println(\"Current page = \" + history.pages.peek());\n    }\n    static void visual(Object object, String type) {}\n    static void point(Object object, String color, int... indices) {}\n}",
